-- title:
--   Adjoining a finite Galois-stable separable set gives a finite Galois extension
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $\Omega$ be a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure making it an algebraic closure of $K$ (the `IsAlgClosure` hypothesis: $\Omega$ is algebraically closed and algebraic over $K$). Let $S \subseteq \Omega$ be a subset which is finite, each of whose elements $x$ is separable over $K$ in the sense of `IsSeparable K x` (that is, $x$ is integral over $K$ with separable minimal polynomial), and which is stable under the full automorphism group: for every $K$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\Omega$ and every $x \in S$ one has $\sigma x \in S$. The conclusion is the conjunction of two assertions about the intermediate field $K(S) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K S` of $\Omega/K$: it is finite-dimensional over $K$, and the extension $K(S)/K$ is Galois, i.e. separable and normal.
--
--   This is the standard criterion identifying the subfield generated by a finite, automorphism-stable set of separable elements of an algebraic closure as a finite Galois extension of the base field; phrasing it for an abstract algebraic closure allows it to be applied to several base fields inside one ambient closure. It is used in the construction of a finite étale Galois cover with $p$-group-controlled Galois action over a discrete valuation ring, in [`IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_finite_etale_isGalois_isPGroup_commutator_le_of_etale`](thm.html#IsDiscreteValuationRing.exists_finite_etale_isGalois_isPGroup_commutator_le_of_etale).
